// src/interpreter/tests/fixtures/plain-orders.js
//
// How people really give orders: plain lines a new player types on a phone,
// with no AI to read them (docs/REVIEW-2026-11.md, D4). Each comes with what
// it should mean, so the grammar's read rate can be watched
// (plain-orders.test.js; `node src/interpreter/tests/fixtures/score.js`
// prints the table).
//
// An entry: { say, want, army?, maps? }
//   say    what he types; lines split by "\n" as the order box does
//   want   'ask'  the right answer is a question back (no order the engine
//                 has fits, or a guess could cost the battle)
//          or a list, one per order he gave:
//            who     unit names; '*' every unit; '*-Legionaries' every unit
//                    but those; '*/second' the second line; '*/first' the rest;
//                    'kind:cavalry', 'kind:archers' (all with bows, slings
//                    or javelins), 'kind:pikes'
//            action  the engine's action, or a list of those that are right
//                    ('defend' is also met by a march that then holds)
//            place   'ground' the map's ground to fight for; 'N', 'NE' ...
//                    that way from where the unit stood; a grid square
//                    ('C5'); 'kind:woods' ground of that kind; 'name:...'
//                    a named place; 'home' toward his own edge
//            fire    'shoot' | 'hold'   enemy: true (an enemy unit)
//            steps   how many steps after the first   when: its condition
//   anyQuestion  with 'ask': the bare "I don't follow" will do (the words
//          are no order at all); else the question must say what is wrong
//   army   'rome' (default), 'macedon', 'parthia'
//   maps   the fields it is tried on (default: all five)
//
// Rome's army: Legionaries, Hastati, Velites (swords, light javelins thrown
// at contact), Praetorian guard; on Greywater a second line of three.

